--- !u!114 &584324656
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 584324651}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: c47ea2746e6af3e4c9c13d9fd3c6e571, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::SelectNation
  leaderSprites:
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: ce5583d9ecdf2634393acf70bd7580e3, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: ef0709743f6d9234db9a850de171de16, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: fc506ab7187b82e45a9ef63333f5b680, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9fb0aac3758281e478c8d156f6010d9b, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: ef272ba0ab34ad94aba0d024ce5a0c9e, type: 3}
  leaderNames:
  - Captain John Smith
  - Samuel de Champlain
  - "HERN\xC1N CORT\xC9S"
  - ADMIRAL MICHIEL DE RUYTER
  - Martim Afonso de Sousa
  leaderSubtitles:
  - Explorer & Colonial Leader
  - Explorer & Founder of New France
  - CONQUEROR OF THE AZTEC EMPIRE & GOVERNOR OF NEW SPAIN
  - EXPLORER & NAVAL COMMANDER
  - EXPLORER & FOUNDER OF BRAZIL'S FIRST CITY
  historicalTexts:
  - "John Smith (c.1580\u20131631) was an English soldier, explorer, and colonial
    leader. In 1607, he joined the Virginia Company expedition that established Jamestown,
    the first permanent English settlement in North America. In 1608, Smith explored
    and mapped the Chesapeake Bay, traveling extensively through its rivers and coastal
    areas. He later became president of Jamestown's governing council, where he organized
    the colony and focused on securing food and maintaining the settlement. His detailed
    map of Virginia was published in 1612 and became an important source for English
    exploration and colonization of the region."
  - "Samuel de Champlain (c.1567\u20131635) was a French explorer, cartographer,
    and colonial leader who played a major role in the establishment of New France.
    He first traveled to North America in 1603, exploring and mapping the St. Lawrence
    River and northeastern coast. In 1608, he founded Qu\xE9bec as a strategic settlement
    on the St. Lawrence River. Champlain developed alliances with Indigenous peoples
    to support French trade and exploration, and continued exploring the interior
    of North America. His exploration, mapping, diplomacy, and leadership helped
    establish the foundations of French colonization in North America."
  - "Hern\xE1n Cort\xE9s (1485\u20131547) was a Spanish conquistador who led an expedition
    that caused the fall of the Aztec Empire and brought large portions of mainland
    Mexico under the rule of the King of Castile in the early 16th century. He was
    awarded the title of Marquess of the Valley of Oaxaca and served as the first
    Governor of New Spain, pioneering Spanish colonial administration on the American
    mainland."
  - "Michiel Adriaenszoon de Ruyter (1607\u20131676) was one of the most famous Dutch
    admirals in history. Serving the Dutch Republic during its Golden Age, he commanded
    fleets in the Anglo-Dutch Wars and led expeditions to North America, the Caribbean,
    and West Africa to secure trade routes and colonial territories for the Dutch
    West India Company (WIC). Renowned for his tactical brilliance, naval raids,
    and protection of merchant convoys, De Ruyter was pivotal in establishing Dutch
    global maritime dominance."
  - "Martim Afonso de Sousa (c. 1500\u20131564) was a Portuguese nobleman and military
    commander. Commissioned by King John III in 1530 to secure and expand Portugal's
    presence in South America, he established Vila de S\xE3o Vicente in 1532, the
    first permanent Portuguese settlement in Brazil, initiating the captaincy system
    for land distribution and colonization. He also explored the coast for Brazilwood
    and metals, playing a crucial role in Portugal's expansion."
